{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>As the heart of the king was good.</b> A singular past-tense verb from the paradigm of מה טבו אהליך (a defective [hollow] root, i.e. טוב with the vav lacking)." "<b>Harvona.</b> Many interpreted [it] as: it was the sword of Haman in haste, for נא [means] like 'now.' Also in the Midrash, that he was Elijah, and the meaning is that he cleaved to good and did as Elijah did. But the truth is that these names mentioned in the scroll are Persian — thus Memukhan, and Esther is translated Hadassah; and if these names were Hebrew, we could not know their meanings, for names are not found according to a single pattern — for here is Bir'a, in which the bet is not a root letter; they said it is from ריעה (friend); and Noaḥ, from ינחמנו (he will comfort us); and Shemuel, from the root of שאל (ask), as I will explain in its place. And the king commanded to show the beauty of the queen — this too is the custom of the land of Edom. And it is possible that Vashti was from a kingdom where the custom of women is to hide, or she thought that he was drunk."], :book :esther, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Kol Sason, Krotoschin, 1840" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001728315"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:29:43.562001307Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}